Stephen Cornell wrote:
> 
> I have a grant to spend up to about 

Here is some of my thoughts.
Now is the time for us number-crunchers to be disappointed.

Digital used to provide the best number-crunchers for the money.
Now Compaq does not deliver their promises.  Intel manufactures
1.5GHz CPUs, while Compaq is still struggling around 833MHz.
In the past, Digital's clock speed used to be about twice as fast as 
those from Intel.

Then, is Intel any better with that higher speed?  No.  
Currently, Intel has not provided a Fortran compiler which 
utilizes SSE2.  Until then, my good old UP2000 is about a factor 
of 1.8 faster than 1.4GHz P4.

Between P4 and Athlon, I would choose P4 because of the RAMBUS
DRAM because many of my codes rely on large matrices which 
do not fit in any cache structure.  I am sure that most 
number crunching applications do need such large matrices.

Finally, I hate the cluster technology.  I would rather 
study physics than all those tricks to use clusters.
Unfortunately, the compaq compiler for ALpha/Linux can't 
do SMP.

Regarding the maximum memory config., P4 and UP2100 are about 
the same.   UP2100 is limited by 2GB.  In the P4 side, practically 
you cannot buy a RAMBUS DRRAM with size larger than 256MB.  So,
practically
you can only go up to 1GB with a P4.

What would I then choose?  Currently, I would choose two P4's than 
a dual-CPU UP2100, hoping that Lahey or Intel will deliver SSE2-capable
compiler for Linux in the near future.  If Compaq delivers a 21364
system right now,
my choice would be different.  I would choose a single-CPU 21364 
than four or six P4's.  (Time is money.)  But that does not seem to
happen 
in the near future, either.

Too bad.....

My last practical advice: just rely on the SPEC numbers, especially
when you don't have time to benchmark anything.  There are not many or
any
better benchmark programs than the SPEC suit.  Many people love to say
"run your own code".   Well, in my case, I have tons of different kinds.


-- 
G. Hugh Song


