I'm currently playing with several AXPpci33 (NoName) motherboards 
fitted with 166 processors: I've built them into two old AT cases, and
have got Redhat 6.2 (kernel 2.2.14-6.0) up and running on both of them.
However, I'm running into a strange problem, and wonder if anyone else
has seen anything resembling it. This was the syndrome:

1) If I booted using the generic boot and root disks, the installation
hung immediately after the dialogue to put in the root password.
However, if I booted a MILO disk from SRM, and then booted the install
set, the installation completed OK. 

2) The machine wouldn't shut down properly: it seemed to hang at the
point where it brought the eth0 interface down 
(in fact, I found that I couldn't stop the eth0 interface even from the
running system.) (As in (1), if I boot via MILO, the shutdown completes
cleanly.)

3) 'make install_modules' also hung (however, again, if I booted using
the MILO disk as a stepping-stone, it worked OK.)

The common feature of (2) and (3) (and I'd be prepared to bet, in (1) as
well) is awk: in each case, a script was trying to run an awk command
which appeared to have hung. The version of awk is gawk 3.0.4: I got
hold of the source and tried building it, having booted directly via SRM
and aboot: 'make test' hung at the first test. I then rebooted using the
MILO disk, and 'make test' completed without a hitch.

Weird. Does it ring any bells with anyone? (Assuming it's not something
funny with my hardware (possible as it's fairly old and untested kit,)
it must be a fairly pervasive problem.)


