This is the most cost-effective solution for this sort of problem,
but 6,000 doesn't go far.  It depends a great deal on what the
memory requirements are (in terms of both size and speed), because
the cheaper boxes tend to have a small amount of slow memory.

Alphas are not currently good value for money, as Compaq seems not
to have developed them in over two years.  Yes, there are lots of
promises, but nothing so far has been delivered.  However, if you
need more than about 2 GB a process, Intel cannot deliver anything.

Pentium 4 systems are likely to perform well on vectorisable
Fortran, as they have quite reasonable memory performance, but
run like a drain on spaghetti code - including clean code for
handling such things as complex graphs, unfortunately.

For such code, Pentium IIIs are as good as anything.


