On 20 Mar 2001 14:23:22 +0000, Stephen Cornell wrote:
> I have a grant to spend up to about �6000 (ex VAT) on a
> number-crunching box.  This machine will be used for long-running
> numerical simulations, and the most important factor is the speed at
> which it runs my own code, written (mostly) in Fortran 90.

Check the quality of the fan(s) on whatever you end up getting - CPU
bound code, especially when it runs from cache, can run the processor
hot.... especially if you have more than one CPU in the box... (MTBF
goes down as the number of components goes up :-()

> (1) Alpha or x86?  
>    -------------- 
> 
> Alpha is always hailed as the best number-crunching CPU out there.  I
> was able to put this to the test with Compaq's `test drive' program.
> I ran some benchmarks using my own code, and found that (i) a Compaq
> E6/466 Mhz processor running True64 ran consistently 3-4 times faster
> than my Pentium II 300 Mhz (ii) Linux on Alpha ran 10-20% slower than
> True64.

With CPU bound code, this sounds like a compiler difference, rather
than anything OS specific. What different compilers are available at
present? (Its been a long time time since I was up on F90 compilers.)
Is your code stable enough that hand-tuning the assembler inner loop
would be worth it? (The best optimising compiler I ever came across
was a guy named Steve - 10 fold speedup on the best available compiler
on a i860 in the early 1990's!)

> I can get a top-spec, dual Xeon/1GHz system which will perform at
> least as well (my code is trivially parallelizable - stochastic
> simulations requiring lots of independent replicates - so performance
> scales with number of CPU's).

What is the memory usage pattern of your code? This is where cache
size will make a huge difference - I used to work at EPCC at Edinburgh
Uni, and the biggest speeds up we got were often down to minimising
cache misses (see above; so much for parallel programming!).

Other thoughts: you say the code is CPU bound, but how big are the
datasets, how are they loaded, check-pointed, saved? The I/O subsystem
will make a big difference... and not just the disks, but also back-up
to elsewhere, tape or server.

-- 

Cheers

Colin

