First of all, without your budget constraints:   ;-)

The Candidates in Question here are: Athlon(x86), P-III(x86) and 21264(Alpha)
First of all, a little table:

CPU/typical system         L1 Cache       L2 Cache            Mainmem
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Athlon/266DDR              64+64          256on-die           64bit
                           @CPUclk        64bit, @CPUclk(?)   @133Mhz DDR

P-III                      16+16          256on-die           64bit
                           @CPUclk        256bit, @CPUclk     @133Mhz DDR

21264(A/B),UP2000(+)       64+64          2-8Mbytes           256bit
                           @CPUclk        1/2 (A)             @83Mhz SDR
                                          1/2 DDR (B)

This is off my head, so take it with a grain of salt ;-). The Mainmem
performance of the x86 machines are typical PC-type Systems, being top-end
today without buring lots of $$$ for fancy things like RamBus on P-III's.
The 'DDR' tag in Mainmem tells you, that DDR Ram is used, therefore giving
you 266Mhz Datatransfers, but 133Mhz Adressclock given to the membus.
The SDR-tag behind the Alpha's membus tells you, that you get a "classic"
single-data-rate bus at 83Mhz, both for adresses and data
The Alpha's come in two "flavours" these days, one is the 21264A, which
has a SDR L2-cache ( with some speedup-"tricks", on which i won't get to
deep into ) clocked at 1/2 CPU clock. The newer 21264B is delivered
at 833Mhz with a 417Mhz DDR L2-cache, that gives you *enormous* Datarates.
Both, Athlon and P-III are available at speeds >= 1Ghz.

To make a decision, CPU clock, L1/L2 configuration and speeds as well as
the memory-subsytems account for the potential execution times in your
app. The Alphas offer ( IMHO ) a superior L2 size combined with a 
better memory subsystem, which is the reason, why i picked those. With
the similar Performance of  both, the Athlon's and the P-III's CPU core
( Athlons are a bit faster than P-III's, especially on FP-intensive code ),
i found a 667-750Mhz 21264A to be as fast as a 1Ghz x86-chip on integer code.

In your case, I would determine the size of the data to be fittet best in
the L2 cache, as well as the overall data-size to be transferred back and
forth to mainmem. If you tend to do much calculation on datasets smaller
than 256Kbytes, then i would recommend an x86. Above that, Alpha kills
any x86, since they are not only crippled by their small L2, but also by
the small memory-bandwidth.


Now with budget ;-(

The Alpha Platform sketched above, is API's UP2000, a full-size ATX
Mainboard, capable of holding 2 21264's. For top-performance, try to
fit a UP2000+ with a single 833Mhz 21264 into you budget.
Unluckily, that will almost guaranteed not be possible. Go down to a 
750Mhz 8Mbytes L2 21264 in the same system. If that still fails, check
the available 667Mhz parts with 4Mbytes L2. However, for that amount
of money, you will be able to get a Dual Athlon config, clocked at 1.2Ghz
per chip. Since your load can be split among both cpu's, they may be a 
better choice here. The dual Athlon will be available, when you spend
the money. The dual boards are about US-$1000, a bit more pricy than
the usual dual P-III, which is not an option ( IMHO ) due to certain
architectural issus ( CPU-mem bus width and clock etc... ).

Linux is ok on the Alpha, as long as you use the compaq compilers, which you
are free to download from their website.

Some random notes: Alpha's can be used headless with a serial console.
That means, they can be build cheap without graphics and monitors, and no
graphics board eats bus cycles. The memory-cost is about the same compared
to the PC's, higher if the PC uses SDR-RAM, lower when the PC's are equipped
with DDR Ram. Memory max is 2Gbyes on the UP2000, and about the same on the
PC's.

I fear, that with you budget, you will end up using a PC, although I suspect
that an Alpha would get your job done quite faster. 

Just my $.02. In case of further questions, feel free.... ;-)


