For your level of communication requirement just buy N boxes and plug
them into an ethernet switch. You don't even have to rack-mount them.
I have a 20 processor PentiumII cluster that is literally just 10 dual
processor boxes sitting on wire-mesh shelves plugged into a hub! (I
also have an IBM supercomputer so I have some idea about the tradeoffs
involved)

> and in adapting my code.  On the other hand, there's nothing stopping

?? From what you say you can probably just take your top level do loop
and divide it up between the processes. All effective parallisation is
high level.
 

From all I've seen a cluster of GHzish dual PIIIs or single Athlons
has to be the way to go unless you can wait for dual Athlons to appear
(they seem to have been "real soon now" for ever). We've just ordered
a "proper beowolf" (oxymoron) and Dual PIIs were more cost-effective
that single Athlons but since you don't need fancy interconnect your
economics may be different.

You don't necessarily need a queueing system if you are going to be
running single jobs (and I agree with what people have said about
those!)

As others have said, you have plenty of good advice at Cambridge (you
might even be able to get somebody to set up MPI for you!), avoid
PetiumIVs and check out your memory speed and cache requirements.

John


