One other thing that is worth bearing in mind are the libraries and
compilers that are available for the various platforms and how good/well
optimised they are for the particular machine.

I've been mainly using fortran on Alphas, but one thing that stands out is
that the Digital/Compaq extended maths libraries are pretty fast - matrix
inversions, fourier transforms and the like run many times faster than
some of the other routines that I've come accross (we've had speed
increases of up to about 20x, although that may just have been that the
previous routines weren't up to much).

I'm are also interested in building a Beowulf cluster and so was wondering
if anyone could suggest a good (fast/reliable) fortran compiler and set of
numerical libraries for x86 processors?

Incidentally, our code is generally both trivially parallel (same program
runs under lots of different conditions) and takes very little input and
produces very little output (either it creates some big matricies in
memory, or does lots of compute cycles).  Hence, I was considering putting
together very basic nodes; just a motherboard, processor (Athlon or Duron,
800-1000MHz), 256MB memory and network card, plus a floppy drive to boot
linux and transfer control to a NFS root directory.  I did a few quick
calculations and reckoned on about 300 UKP for a node.

Andy.


