|> filesystems with which NQS has problems?

Not a clue, I am afraid.  But I can tell you what it assumes and
relies upon.  I am indebted to Michael Rutter for locating the
reasons for NQS's aberrations.

The authors (quite reasonably) wanted guaranteed update semantics,
but Unix did not and does not provide them (not even with sync,
fsync, flush, Old Uncle Tom Cobbley and all).  They discovered
that inodes were being atomically updated on the systems they used,
and they jumped to the conclusion that this was a stable feature
of Unix.  So so the geniuses stored lock data in the last modified
time of the inodes of various files (and possibly other fields).

In addition to playing Old Hob with file system scanning utilities
(including some forms of dumping), not least because dates of 2028
are unlikely to be correct, they are quite simply wrong that inode
update always bypasses Unix's usual file block caching code.  This
is the reason that you absolutely must NOT put the NQS directories
onto an NFS mounted partition, and that it won't work with IBM GPFS
and a good many other fancy, distributed filing systems.

I do not know how likely it is to corrupt its own data structures
if there is a power cut (surely not?) while inodes have been only
partially written back but, given its other misdesigns, it probably
will.  And, for the same reason, the only practical way to recover
from such corruption is to reinstall NQS and lose all queued jobs
and output.


Regards,
Nick Maclaren,
University of Cambridge Computing Service,
