(this starts to become OT for Alpha, but here is the number
crunchers' group!)
I have a 700 MHz Dual PIII running Linux. Our group develops an
analog circuit simulator (70% Fortran 77, the rest is C).
We are always in demand for good compilers for that and we purchased
the Portland Group C/Fortran compilers, because they give about 20-30% more
performance than gcc/g77 for our code.
This pays, because the runtime may be days to weeks.

I found the PGI compilers to be rather stable, but i have to warn you:
- STMicroelectronics has acquired Portland Group.
  see: http://www.pgroup.com/stpgi_announce.htm
  I assume, the people at PGI will have to work on compilers for embedded
  processors from now on and will not concentrate on improving their Intel
  compilers.
- There has been no release since Oct 2000.
- All my bug reports were answered and confirmed, but no fix in sight.
- The compilers do not produce working progs, which rely on correct 
  NaN handling (e.g. nan .eq. nan is true).

I found gcc/g77 to be much more relieable for my code than PGI.
I would be very interested to hear some thoughts
and experiences on other Linux compilers.

I hope that helps,
Martin.

