Anthony,

I have nearly an identical system.  However, rather than the C++ compilers, 
I use the Compaq FORTRAN compilers.  I have found the following procedures 
work well

1) make sure all the dependencies are satisfied up front.  This is tricky 
because dpkg usually does this for you.  rpm does not do as well a job.

2) remove all previous versions of the Compaq software.  Again, dpkg 
usually does this for you.  Installing a new rpm package over another, will 
not give you a clean system.

3) install the rpm packages in the right order using the rpm program as 
installed by a dpkg system (part of the Debian 2.2r2 system).  Use the 
options to force the install and ignore the dependencies.  rpm will look 
for other rpm packages not installed (hence, item 1 above).  I don't have 
all the switches in front of me.

4) If you need to upgrade (as I recently learned the hard way with the new 
beta versions from Compaq), then start back to item 1, not item 3.

This seems to work for me.  Yes, the software does start by answering the 
license question, then never asks again.  I also own the commercial license 
for the FORTRAN ($300).  I am not sure how that applies anymore for the 
upgrades I have installed.

Maybe Compaq/New H, will get a clue and build Debian packages.

