The 433a and the 433au are very similar machines.  The "au" means unix or 
NT, while the "a" means NT only.  But like I said, they're so similar, that 
it doesn't take much to get unix or linux running on an "a."

Somebody else will tell you more details about "a" vs "au," but I can't.

Here's one of the cool things about Alphas:  The SRM console.  It's much 
better than the bios junk you're used to in x86.  You should read the faqs, 
and specifically read the stuff about Alphabios vs SRM console.  Most 
likely, you want to use SRM instead of alphabios, but that detail might be 
one of the caveats of "a" vs "au."
    	http://www.newfieldinn.com/faq/ 
    	http://www.alphalinux.org/ 
    	http://www.tru64.org/ 

I suggest you also read about the Jumpstart CD.

I suggest you obtain the RH 7.0 CD instead of 6.2, although IMHO 7.0 isn't 
all that much better.  It does install easier, see below.
You need to be sure your CD set is meant for Alpha (aka AXP, see faqs), not 
i386 or whatever.

If you decide not to get the 7.0 cd, this information might be helpful:

From SRM console, the boot command for RH 6.2 is
  (assuming your cd is dka4 -- type "sho dev d" to find out)
  (assuming you have a graphics adapter and local keyb, mouse, monitor)
  >>> b dka4 -fi kernels/generic.gz -fl root=/dev/scd0

From SRM console, the boot command for RH 7.0 is
  (assuming the same as above)
  >>> b dka4 -fl 0


