Normally the PWS 433a is delivered without a SCSI-controller. If you got 
one, be lucky, because if you want to use Ture64 Unix you need one to boot. 
And Ture64 boots not with every controller as far as I have heard.
If you are unsure about if you have a controller or not,  the alphabios 
shows you what harddisk (and cdrom) config you have got.
If you want to switch to the SRM console (don't do that without a bit 
documentation about it), you can do this by entering the CMOS-Setup in 
alphabios and choosing 'advanced settings' (by pressing a F-key).
There you find a menu point console (or something like that). You have to 
change it from alpha bios to unix console. Then you have to switch your PWS 
off and to wait 10 seconds. After starting again you will be in the SRM 
console. Type 'help' and yopu will get the commands listet you can use.
If you want to get back to alphabios type 'arc'.

I don't konw much about the Redhat distribution (I use SuSE 7.0, download 
on ftp://ftp.suse.com/pub/suse/AXP/), but I didn't managed it to boot from 
CD with AlphaBios (only with SRM). So I suggest to make bootdisks and try 
to install RH70 again.
For booting from floppy you have to set the parameters in the bios 
(F2->Setup->UTILITIES->'OS Selection Setup') as follows (Milo 2.0.35):

Boot Name: Boot_linux_from_Floppy
Boot File: A:  \linload.exe
OS Path: CD: \
OS Options: boot fd0:/vmlinux.gz root=/dev/fd0 fake_initrd

For booting from harddisk, you should notice that if you use MILO, you need 
an FAT16 partion on your harddisk mounted as /boot (for example my 
configuration: /dev/hdc1 FAT16 /boot (klein)    /dev/hdc2 LinuxSwap (128MB) 
/dev/hdc3 /root (rest) ). The OS Setup looks like that:

Boot Name: Boot_linux_from_Disk
Boot File: Disk 3:  \linload.exe
OS Path: CD: \
OS Options: boot hdc1:/vmlinux.gz root=/dev/hdc2

I hope I rembered everithing right and it can help you.

