I have a grant to spend up to about =A36000 (ex VAT) on a
number-crunching box.  This machine will be used for long-running
numerical simulations, and the most important factor is the speed at
which it runs my own code, written (mostly) in Fortran 90.  The grant
doesn't become active for a couple of months, but I thought I'd start
making plans now.  I also need to be able to run some Microsoft
software, as well as the usual suspects of Latex, Emacs, etc., but I
could use my 3-year-old Pentium II machine for that and buy a headless
calculating machine.  On the other hand, if I opt for an x86 machine
then I get a much nicer desktop for free, as it were.  This new box
will, of course, be running some form of *nix.

The obvious choices seem to be Alpha or x86 architecture.  Here's how
far I've got.

(1) Alpha or x86?  =

   -------------- =


Alpha is always hailed as the best number-crunching CPU out there.  I
was able to put this to the test with Compaq's `test drive' program.
I ran some benchmarks using my own code, and found that (i) a Compaq
E6/466 Mhz processor running True64 ran consistently 3-4 times faster
than my Pentium II 300 Mhz (ii) Linux on Alpha ran 10-20% slower than
True64.  These ratios were more or less the same for 32-bit Integer
code as for 64-bit floating-point, and also agree roughly with the
benchmarks that I found on Compaq's web site.  I was careful to try
several different compiler options in all cases, so as to get the best
performance.  My code is typically cpu-bound rather than IO-bound, so
I expect performance to scale with processor speed.  Interestingly,
the IA-64/666 in the test drive didn't perform any better than my
Pentium II (taking into account the clock speed).

It looks as though, for my purposes, an E6/466 is probably equivalent
to a Pentium III/1000 (assuming the Pentium III is no faster than a
Pentium III), and a Pentium 4/1.5 GHz is probably the equal of an
E6/667.  However, the x86 option is significantly cheaper than the
Alpha one.  An XP1000 workstation costs around =A36000 (this was, in
fact, the basis for the sum in the grant proposal), and for =A32000 less
I can get a top-spec, dual Xeon/1GHz system which will perform at
least as well (my code is trivially parallelizable - stochastic
simulations requiring lots of independent replicates - so performance
scales with number of CPU's).  Moreover, all software for Alpha (such
as Matlab, Mathematica) is more expensive than for Linux/x86.

So, I'd be grateful for advice on the following:

* Is the above analysis flawed?  Have I overlooked a good reason for
  going for the Alpha?  =


* Is there anyone selling dual-CPU Alpha-based hardware at less than
  =A36000 in the UK?  http://www.alphalinux.org/hardware/vendors.shtml
  didn't come up trumps.

(2) Which X86?  =

   ----------- =


Assuming that the X86 is the better bet, which should I go for?  The
fastest CPUs appear to be Pentium 4 or AMD Athlon, but I haven't seen
any such machines with more than one processor.  Pentium III come in
vanilla and Xeon flavours, but I haven't found any documentation
saying what benefit Xeon brings (unless one opts for the prohibitively
expensive higher-cache options).  Also, the different RAM options are
highly confusing: some Pentium III machines are sold with RDRAM, but
does this give any benefit over SDRAM if the system bus runs at only
133 MHz?

So, can anyone help me with the following:

* How important is it to opt for high-speed memory, especially when
  the system bus runs at 133 MHz?

* Are there hardware vendors in the UK selling 2-way Pentium 4 or AMD
  Athlon systems?  What about affordable 4-way systems?

* Is it worth my while going for the Xeon option?

* which processor gives the best performance for my money?  The Compaq
  benchmarks I mentioned before suggest that the Athlon may be a
  better bet than a Pentium III at the same clock speed.


I also have money to buy a new laptop, but that's a different story...
