Those arent' error codes.  They are checkpoints, so in the event of an 
actual failure, you have some information about what caused the failure.  
The idea is similar to scattering printf statements throughout a C program 
that is buggy and crashing.

If the machine boots after showing those numbers, that means there was no 
error.  If there were an error, the machine would either crash or display a 
prompt.  If you're unfamiliar with SRM, I suggest looking through the faqs 
at
http://www.newfieldinn.com/faq/ 
http://www.alphalinux.org/ 
http://www.tru64.org/ 


